          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              In terms of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
              this may be understood as follows:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝔽
                          %mi p
                        %mo =
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_p = \mathbb{Z}/(p \mathbb{Z})
                  is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+of+functions"} ring of functions
                  exactly on the point
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∈
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p)\in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spec%28Z%29"} Spec(Z)
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_p
                  is the functions on the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+neighbourhood"} formal neighbourhood
                  of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p)
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_{(p)}
                  is the ring of functions defined on the open complement of the point
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p)
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+neighbourhood"} open neighbourhood
            %p
              So the localization at the Moore spectrum of one of these rings localizes to the formal neighbourhood of their support. For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_p
              the support is the closed point, and so the localization there is infinitesimally bigger than that, as given by the
              = succeed "-adic" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_{(p)}
              is open and hence contains its own formal neighbourhood, so localizing here just gives the plain localization.
          %h3#basic_properties Basic properties
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fracture+theorem"} fracture theorem
          %h3#in_chromatic_homotopy_theory In chromatic homotopy theory
          %p
            Many of the basic constructions and theorems in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+homotopy+theory"} chromatic homotopy theory
            apply to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+spectrum"} finite
            = succeed "-local" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %ul
